</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The systematic violations of proportional hazards assumptions for key semantic drift features (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>) provide strong empirical justification for our multi-paradigm modeling approach. These violations suggest that the effect of semantic drift on failure hazard changes over time, supporting the use of AFT models that naturally accommodate time-varying effects.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Feature Importance and Risk Factor Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5.4 Feature Importance and Risk Factor Analysis ‣ 5 Results ‣ Time-To-Inconsistency: A Survival Analysis of Large Language Model Robustness to Adversarial Attacks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes model–drift interactions from the advanced Cox PH (HRs; dashed line <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math> neutral effect). Three patterns emerge:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02712v1/semantic_drift_effects.svg" id="S5.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:384/245;" width="384" height="245"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Semantic Drift Effects on Failure Risk: p2p drift consistently increases failure risk (HR <math id="S5.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> 2), while cumulative drift often provides protection (HR <math id="S5.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> 1), suggesting sophisticated adaptation mechanisms across models.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Prompt-to-prompt (p2p) drift is catastrophic.</span>
Acute turn-to-turn shifts (<span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p2p</span>) are the dominant failure driver across all LLMs, with large hazard ratios: <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{HR}\!\approx\!1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>HR</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">≈</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{HR}\!\approx\!1.2</annotation></semantics></math> (Gemini), <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.3</annotation></semantics></math> (gpt_oss), <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.6</annotation></semantics></math> (gpt_4o), <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.2</annotation></semantics></math> (llama_4), <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.8</annotation></semantics></math> (qwen). Thus, even small immediate semantic jumps sharply elevate next-turn failure risk, especially for gpt_4o and llama_4.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Context-to-prompt drift poses moderate risk.</span>
Deviations from the running context (<span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">c2p</span>) are harmful but weaker than p2p, with <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{HR}\!\approx\!1.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>HR</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">≈</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{HR}\!\approx\!1.1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.1</annotation></semantics></math> across models. This suggests failures are more sensitive to sudden shocks than to gradual divergence from the cumulative context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Cumulative drift is protective.</span>
Counterintuitively, higher accumulated drift (<span id="S5.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cum</span>) is associated with <em id="S5.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lower</em> risk (all <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{HR}\!&lt;\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>HR</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{HR}\!&lt;\!1</annotation></semantics></math>, roughly <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math>). A plausible interpretation is adaptation: once a conversation has survived several shifts, the model may stabilize to the evolving topic (or adversarial pressure decays), reducing incremental hazard.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, p2p emerges as the principal actionable risk factor (acute shocks), c2p as a secondary risk (context divergence), and cumulative drift as a resilience marker. These effects are consistent in sign across models but vary in magnitude, revealing distinct vulnerability profiles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Temporal Failure Patterns</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our survival curve analysis reveals distinct failure patterns across different risk strata. High-risk conversations (top quartile of cumulative drift) exhibit a median survival time of 4.2 rounds, while low-risk conversations maintain coherence for 7.8+ rounds on average.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Risk Stratification Analysis: Median Survival Times by Model</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low Risk</span></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Medium Risk</span></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High Risk</span></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Log-Rank p</span></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hazard Ratio</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Cox Baseline</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">7.8+</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">6.2</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">4.2</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">2.34</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Cox Advanced</td>
